// [actually|really|literally] is optional and consumes a filler word
// wedged between the lead-in phrase and the actual name ("my name is
// actually Ana") so the captured star is just the name — not "Actually
// Ana" (confirmed by testing this was happening before). Also confirmed
// a name that's literally "Actually" still falls through correctly to
// the trailing _, since RiveScript backtracks when consuming the
// optional word would leave nothing for the required _.
//
// Only the FIRST word of whatever follows is kept as the name — a
// trailing surname/middle name is deliberately dropped ("my name is Ana
// Silva" -> "Ana", confirmed by testing), per product decision to store
// first names only. The trailing _ captures that first word into star2;
// the final [*] just consumes (without capturing — confirmed against
// the real engine: an unmatched trailing [*] reads back as the literal
// string "undefined" through <starN>, so it's never referenced) any
// remaining words so the trigger still matches the full message.
+ (my name is|it is|i am|im|you can call me|call me) [actually|really|literally] _ [*]
* <get name> == <formal> => <set unknownStreak=0><set userIntention=givename><set botIntention=<bot intent_givename>>We already know each other, <get name>! How can I help today?
- <set unknownStreak=0><set userIntention=givename><set botIntention=<bot intent_givename>>Nice to meet you, {formal}<star2>{/formal}.<set name={formal}<star2>{/formal}> How can I help you today?

// Mid-conversation correction ("actually, call me Ana") — the trigger
// above requires the lead-in phrase at the very start of the message, so
// a preamble breaks it. Only widened for "call me"/"my name is" (not
// "i am"/"im" too, which are common enough mid-sentence that a leading
// wildcard risks false positives) — explicit correction wording instead
// of a bare leading [*], to keep the match intentional. Same optional
// filler-word slot as above, and same first-word-only capture (trailing
// _ [*]) as the main lead-in trigger above.
+ (actually|wait|no|sorry) [*] (my name is|call me|you can call me) [actually|really|literally] _ [*]
* <get name> == <formal> => <set unknownStreak=0><set userIntention=givename><set botIntention=<bot intent_givename>>We already know each other, <get name>! How can I help today?
- <set unknownStreak=0><set userIntention=givename><set botIntention=<bot intent_givename>>Got it, {formal}<star3>{/formal}.<set name={formal}<star3>{/formal}> How can I help you today?

// % (Previous) block: catches a BARE name reply — just "Ana", no "my name
// is" prefix — that the trigger above can't, since it requires one of
// those lead-in phrases. Scoped to the bot's last line asking for a name
// (either phrasing: the initial ask or the repeat-greeting nudge), so it
// only activates in that exact context — RiveScript checks % matches
// before any normal trigger, so an unscoped version of this would
// swallow any single-word message (confirmed by testing: it captured
// "skills" as if it were a name). Rather than exclude every existing
// single-word CV trigger (fragile, needs updating every time one's
// added), it asks for confirmation instead — safe regardless of whether
// the word happens to collide with real vocabulary.
//
// [*] not *, on both sides: * requires 1+ trailing characters, but "...
// what should i call you" has nothing after it once the "?" is stripped
// — a bare * there never matches (confirmed by testing: it silently
// fell through to the generic fallback). [*] allows zero-width, so it
// still matches when there's nothing left to consume.
//
// Greeting words are excluded and redirected back to the real trigger
// (<get ctrl_greet> already handles a repeat "hi" with its own nudge) —
// otherwise a second "hi" gets treated as a candidate name instead of a
// greeting (confirmed by testing: "Is Hi your name?").
// "call me Ana" / "im Ana" / "it is Ana" right after the bot asks for a
// name is indistinguishable, by word count alone, from a bare two- or
// three-word name ("Ana Silva") to the _ / _ _ / _ _ _ blocks below —
// that's the reported bug: it got captured whole as the name ("Is Call
// Me Ana your name?", confirmed by testing before this fix). This
// trigger matches the lead-in phrase explicitly via @namelead, so its
// pattern text is an "_"-containing (alpha) match same as the bare
// blocks below, but at the same word count it sorts ahead of them by
// raw pattern length (confirmed against the real engine — RiveScript.js
// sorts same-word-count alpha triggers by trigger-text length,
// descending, and this one is textually longer) and so wins the %
// priority race. It redirects into the real lead-in trigger further
// down this file (via a canonical "my name is <name>" phrasing) so only
// the name is used and the reply logic isn't duplicated here.
+ [*] @namelead _
% [*] (what should i call you|you can tell me your name) [*]
- {@ my name is <star1>}

+ _
% [*] (what should i call you|you can tell me your name) [*]
* <star> == hi => {@ <star>}
* <star> == hey => {@ <star>}
* <star> == hello => {@ <star>}
* <star> == howdy => {@ <star>}
* <star> == yo => {@ <star>}
- <set unknownStreak=0><set userIntention=confirmname><set botIntention=<bot intent_confirmname>><set pendingname={formal}<star>{/formal}>Is <get pendingname> your name?

// Same idea, for a two-word bare name ("Madelaine Kincaid") — `_` above
// only matches a single word, so a first+last name fell through to the
// generic name-parse-failure reply (confirmed by testing). Widening the
// one-word block to `*` was tried and rejected: since %-Previous always
// wins over normal triggers, it swallowed EVERYTHING said right after
// "What should I call you?", including "my name is Bob", "call me Bob",
// and even an unrelated question like "what are your skills" (confirmed
// by testing — each got wrongly asked "is that your name?"). An exact
// two-word block avoids that: it only fires for precisely two bare words.
//
// Only <star1> (the first word) is used for pendingname below — <star2>
// is still captured (needed for the good morning/afternoon/evening
// exclusion checks) but deliberately not referenced in the name itself,
// per product decision to store first names only ("Madelaine Kincaid"
// -> "Madelaine", confirmed by testing).
+ _ _
% [*] (what should i call you|you can tell me your name) [*]
* <star1> <star2> == good morning => {@ good morning}
* <star1> <star2> == good afternoon => {@ good afternoon}
* <star1> <star2> == good evening => {@ good evening}
- <set unknownStreak=0><set userIntention=confirmname><set botIntention=<bot intent_confirmname>><set pendingname={formal}<star1>{/formal}>Is <get pendingname> your name?

// Same idea again, for a three-word bare name ("Ana Maria Silva"). No
// known 3-word greeting phrase exists in the greet trigger's array to
// exclude here (checked), so no redirect conditions needed like the
// two-word block above. Same first-word-only rule as the two-word block:
// <star2>/<star3> are captured but not used in pendingname.
+ _ _ _
% [*] (what should i call you|you can tell me your name) [*]
- <set unknownStreak=0><set userIntention=confirmname><set botIntention=<bot intent_confirmname>><set pendingname={formal}<star1>{/formal}>Is <get pendingname> your name?

// {formal} is regex-processed before <set>/<get> tags resolve, so
// {formal}<get pendingname>{/formal} would format the literal text
// "<get pendingname>" instead of its value (confirmed by testing — it
// printed "undefined"). <star> is safe inside {formal} because it's
// substituted earlier, before {formal} runs — so pendingname is
// formatted once at capture time above, then reused plain below.
// Word lists are shared (begin.rive's yeswords/nowords/maybewords), not
// retyped here — same lists cv.rive's experiencefollowup block uses.
//
// This more specific trigger (yes + a nickname-lead phrase) is tried
// before the plain one below, since RiveScript sorts %-scoped triggers by
// specificity same as normal ones — a bare "yes"/"yes it is" has no
// nicknamelead match and falls through unchanged (confirmed by testing).
// Trailing _ [*] (not a bare *): only the first word after the
// nickname-lead phrase is kept as the name override — a trailing
// surname is dropped, same first-word-only rule as every other
// name-capture trigger in this file (confirmed by testing).
+ (@yeswords) [*] (@nicknamelead) _ [*]
% is * your name
- <set unknownStreak=0><set userIntention=givename><set botIntention=<bot intent_givename>><set name={formal}<star3>{/formal}>Nice to meet you, <get name>. How can I help you today?

+ (@yeswords) [*]
% is * your name
